    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer; all devices participate in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts* (whole FFN blocks), while attention and other dense layers are typically replicated (or sharded via TP/DP on top).\n\nIn practice, production systems combine them: e.g., TP within a node (NVLink domain) and EP across nodes, plus data parallelism (DP) for replicated copies to increase throughput. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP inference, their data flow, the collectives they rely on, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention: $Y = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V \\cdot W_O$\n- MLP: $Y = \\text{gelu}(X W_1) W_2$\n\nMegatron-style TP shards as follows:\n\n- **Column-parallel**: $W_1$, $W_Q$, $W_K$, $W_V$ are split along the output dimension: $W_1 = [W_1^{(0)}; W_1^{(1)}; \\dots; W_1^{(p-1)}]$ where $p$ = TP degree. Each rank computes a slice of the intermediate activation.\n- **Row-parallel**: $W_2$, $W_O$ are split along the input",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
